#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510502} :activity/id #uuid "edcee70a-6434-5e86-b8e7-971648d37f3e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049866 :topic/id #uuid "19a3820a-0bf7-5130-9182-e23d96b83145" :topic/math-academy-id 3298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fea37df-e66f-522e-b62b-c4201b1b4816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509719} {:answer/id #uuid "23e3d9b1-9b71-54ad-a4bf-163a871b7d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509765} {:answer/id #uuid "62f9b791-9810-563a-bab9-7a82307110c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509894} {:answer/id #uuid "91ed605b-90ba-53ea-9dfb-1121054ab8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509998} {:answer/id #uuid "dd43de8d-951e-5173-a7ee-0e8b7890925f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509998} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcbeeea1-2823-5cdd-8385-469893fd88bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5acbf13f-ff67-5449-9947-a8679cb075b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159664" :question/problem "The scores of girls and boys on a national post-high school exam have standard deviations of $3$ points and $4$ points, respectively. The authorities want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the girls' scores is the same as the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the boys' scores. They construct a $95$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nOfficials sampled $111$ girls and $115$ boys and found that their mean scores on the test were $74$ points and $73.4$ points, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.6\\pm 0.92.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=74-73.6=0.6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{111}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{115}}≈0.920\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 0.920\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.6\\pm 0.920.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.6-0.92,\\,0.6+0.92)=(-0.32,\\,1.52)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "517bedd8-acc9-5598-9606-561a1e7bcb04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016ded86-d025-503c-b985-6d6426861714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 0.701" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509697} {:answer/id #uuid "068bb060-b53b-584e-bc05-e9b1925f5bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 0.747" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509705} {:answer/id #uuid "37885ebf-04e6-5d47-8e02-92e0f6c71755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509808} {:answer/id #uuid "b5897ae1-26f4-5f76-a208-ed3f569c0dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.649" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510078} {:answer/id #uuid "e7086e67-8297-5052-9066-130384411f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509808} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a48ccb-a74e-541e-97fa-2294603b02e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f9f9f92-90ae-5c14-9ede-c5718a2ba70e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159628" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=54$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=77$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=23$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=12,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=23-12=11\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.7}^{2}}{54}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{77}}≈1.344\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 11\\pm 1.344\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5207b2ac-ba48-5823-9d3d-fc02b606d266" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191026ef-026f-56e1-aed9-51375e82a905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509738} {:answer/id #uuid "39af70a3-b7c9-5157-a843-61baeb776152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509812} {:answer/id #uuid "86b3b730-b022-5e9e-ae72-0ac285e66745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509981} {:answer/id #uuid "adadc4f6-e872-59e0-a4f8-ce8c2ef02108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510061} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f6975c-69e9-5183-8d49-09c3be9f6789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509738} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb63ba3-c644-5698-927f-01d281fe6523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a0faeac4-bc51-5e0f-965c-b834d48291a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159636" :question/problem "The lengths of traditional baguettes and brown baguettes made at a particular French bakery have standard deviations of $4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $3\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively. The baker wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the lengths of traditional baguettes is larger than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the lengths of brown baguettes. He constructs a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the baker sampled $50$ traditional baguettes and $40$ brown baguettes and found that their mean lengths were $65\\,\\text{cm}$ and $63.5\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1.5\\pm 1.421.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=65-63.5=1.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{40}}≈1.214\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1.5\\pm 1.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1.5\\pm 1.214.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1.5-1.214,\\,1.5+1.214)=(0.286,\\,2.714)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a2ed7b82-21f1-5f47-b31c-6616cabb005c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341e9984-edd6-50e2-bcfc-5f3f669d286d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509798} {:answer/id #uuid "41f44e83-0d9c-596d-b75d-78d997dc828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509827} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a8c787-0565-563f-b6ab-1b60b46da41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510041} {:answer/id #uuid "bda6715a-bd7f-5810-9508-0c32ee7cc877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510088} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc53a9-6a52-56d2-9347-e34017750f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509798} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a3ec7b-79e1-5862-a616-14f2961b8754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5b6883ab-506f-535a-b78d-aa1454cc2ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159667" :question/problem "A study of body weight conducted on two age groups of women, X and Y, yields standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=8\\,\\text{lb}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=10\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively.\nA researcher wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of group X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of group Y. She decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe researcher sampled $50$ women from group X and $55$ from group Y, finding that their mean weights are $58\\,lb$ and $64\\,lb,$  respectively. Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-6\\pm 2.895.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=58-64=-6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{8}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{10}^{2}}{55}}≈2.895\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -6\\pm 2.895\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-6\\pm 2.895.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-6-2.895,\\,-6+2.895)=(-8.895,\\,-3.105)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "eeaf510f-334c-5379-92ee-488a7974c6bf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff10644-8d80-59d2-847c-acdc62e1d31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509856} {:answer/id #uuid "5737153b-7255-54c5-9ed1-76b4f29f597d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.837" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509869} {:answer/id #uuid "61672d44-76c1-5941-96f9-b547c24560e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.304" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509890} {:answer/id #uuid "b148fa07-0393-5190-a3cf-99f1bae95ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510070} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b14202-3b4b-5788-bb0b-55e0f3678b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 0.587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509890} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4d1d6d-2c26-56bc-a27d-991d70114cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33a01272-f3c9-5c47-b349-cebcb033572c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159630" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4.6$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=73$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=33$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=33-26=7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4.6}^{2}}{82}+\\frac{{5.2}^{2}}{73}}≈1.304\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 7\\pm 1.304\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9b44149-b78f-586f-aef5-56e50d096b88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510529}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Known and Unequal Population Variances" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509691}]}
